Git Pull:
git pull origin main → Downloads the latest changes from the remote repository and merges them into the local branch.

When to use:
Before starting work, pull the latest changes to keep the local repository updated.

Git Fetch:
git fetch → Downloads the latest remote changes/metadata without merging them into the local branch.

Metadata → Data about data.

When to use:
Use git fetch to check the latest remote changes before pulling.
It is especially useful when working with branches.

Git Pull vs Git Fetch:

git fetch → Downloads changes only; does not merge.
git pull → Downloads changes and merges them.

Branching:
A branch is a separate copy/line of development created from the main branch.

Why use branches?
- Multiple developers can work on the same project safely.
- Changes in a branch do not directly affect the main branch.
- Developers can work on different tasks independently.
- A Pull Request (PR) can be created to review changes before merging into main.

Example:

main
  ↓
feature-1
feature-2
feature-3

Each developer can work on their own branch without directly changing the main branch.

Pull Request (PR):
A Pull Request is used to submit changes from a branch for review before merging them into the main branch.

Branching helps prevent developers from overwriting or losing each other's work.